 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "How it doubles",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Four CMOS switches, S1 to S4, work in two phases. With S2 and S4 on, the flying capacitor C1 charges to V+. Then S2 and S4 open and S1 and S3 close, stacking C1 on top of V+, so the output reaches 2 × V+ with no load. Under load the output sags by the switch on-resistance, the capacitor ESR and the charge lost between capacitors."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output model",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The doubler output behaves as a 2 × V+ source in series with ROUT, which depends on the switch on-resistance, the oscillator frequency and the size and ESR of C1 and C2. Large, low-ESR capacitors lower both the output resistance and the ripple."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shutdown",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "SD at ground: normal operation. SD above 40 % of the V+ voltage: the part stops and its supply current falls to about 1 µA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Start-up diode",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The oscillator draws its supply from OUT to GND and needs more than 1.8 V there to run. In the doubler a Schottky diode from the input to OUT charges the output at power-up so the oscillator starts, and keeps the chip's internal parasitic diode from conducting and possibly latching up. It has to carry the current that charges the output capacitor at start-up and have a low forward drop. A 1N5817 suits most designs; with an input ramp under 10 V/ms a smaller MBR0520LT1 will do."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitor choice",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "C1 and C2 are chosen from the allowed voltage drop (IOUT × ROUT), the allowed ripple and the target efficiency. Low-ESR parts give higher efficiency, less drop and less ripple."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Paralleling",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Any number of LM2665s can run in parallel to cut the output resistance. Each needs its own flying capacitor; one output capacitor serves them all."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cascading",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Stacking stages gives higher voltage, but each extra stage costs efficiency and adds output resistance and ripple, which limits how many stages are practical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Regulated output",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "An LDO such as the LP2980-5.0 after the doubler gives a regulated rail; the -3.3, -3.0 and -ADJ versions give other voltages. The two input-limit inequalities both have to hold at the worst case."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Divider mode",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Swapping input and output turns the doubler into a precise divide-by-two. The input sits across OUT and GND, which are the oscillator's supply rails, so no start-up diode is needed; each switch then blocks only VIN/2, so the input may go up to 11 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power supply",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The input supply, 2.5 V to 5.5 V, has to be well regulated and able to deliver the input current. A supply far from the chip may need bulk capacitance beside the ceramic bypass capacitors."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Fast switching and large pulse currents make layout matter. CIN goes on the same side as the chip, as close as possible, with short wide traces to V+ and GND, to keep switching spikes off V+. COUT goes on the same side, close to OUT and GND, with short wide traces; the CIN and COUT returns meet at one point next to the GND pin. C1, the flying capacitor, also sits on the chip's side, close, with short wide traces to CAP+ and CAP-."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "ESD protection on chip is limited: store and handle with the leads shorted or in conductive foam."
  }
 ],
